{
  "global_card_id": "smart_money",
  "global_canonical": "Smart Money",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "SMC",
  "aliases": [
    "big players",
    "institutional traders",
    "Institutional Money",
    "Institutional Traders",
    "Large Players"
  ],
  "definition": "Smart Money refers to large institutional traders (banks, hedge funds) who use their capital to manipulate price and engineer liquidity. They trigger retail stop-loss clusters and create false patterns via liquidity grabs or sweeps, generating the opposing orders needed to build or execute large positions at favorable prices. Their true directional intent is revealed through market structure, with price typically reversing once liquidity has been collected.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Extra-Supplement": "Institutional traders that manipulate price to engineer liquidity, creating artificial patterns to trigger retail stop losses and entries. This provides opposing orders to fill large positions. Their directional intent is revealed through market structure (higher highs/lows or lower highs/lows).",
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement": "Large financial institutions that manipulate markets by triggering retail traders' stop-losses, creating liquidity pools to execute their positions. They deceive traders into false pattern recognition, then reverse price after collecting liquidity.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "Institutional traders (e.g., banks, hedge funds) with large capital who influence price by triggering retail stop-loss clusters through liquidity grabs or sweeps, creating liquidity to build or execute large positions at favorable prices."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Establish directional bias from market structure (HH/HL for bullish, LH/LL for bearish) as smart money's footprint; SMC-Indicator also looks for a break of structure followed by retracement forming equal highs/lows.",
    "Identify obvious retail liquidity pools where stop-losses accumulate: trend lines, equal highs/lows, support/resistance.",
    "Watch for a sweep of a liquidity pool opposite the bias (manipulation) followed by a strong move in the bias direction (initiation); confirm price moves beyond stop levels before reversing.",
    "Distinguish a liquidity grab (single long-wick candle that breaks S/R then immediately reverses into the prior range, a false breakout) from a liquidity sweep (multi-candle push through the zone before a gradual reversal indicating accumulation) — emphasized by SMC-Strong-Weak.",
    "Reversal candles often display long wicks and small bodies (e.g., hammer, shooting star) after the grab/sweep — emphasized by SMC-Strong-Weak.",
    "After initiation/sweep, look for price to fill an imbalance (fair value gap) and pull back to an order block or demand/supply zone as a probable smart money entry zone."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Ignoring market structure context and trading solely on visible liquidity levels, entering against the true smart money direction.",
    "Entering before a liquidity sweep is confirmed, risking stops being taken; assuming price must immediately reach a liquidity pool without awaiting the manipulation-initiation sequence.",
    "Treating a zone or point of interest as valid without prior liquidity clearance.",
    "Misinterpreting a liquidity grab or sweep (or trendline/equal high-low break) as a genuine breakout and trading the initial move without reversal confirmation.",
    "Getting trapped by the second leg of a multi-candle sweep push before the reversal — emphasized by SMC-Strong-Weak.",
    "Confusing a liquidity grab with a Change of Character (CHoCH) by not waiting for the candle to close beyond the structure level — emphasized by SMC-Strong-Weak."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Align with smart money by trading only after retail stop-losses have been swept, entering in the direction of the institutional move to avoid retail traps. SMC-Extra and SMC-Indicator emphasize taking entries on pullbacks to points of interest (order blocks, fair value gaps, demand/supply zones) once a sweep confirms the bias, targeting structural liquidity levels for high-probability, risk-managed trades. SMC-Strong-Weak is more specific on execution: enter on the reversal after a confirmed grab/sweep (e.g., on candle close), place stop-losses beyond the wick or swept level, and target the next structural zone with a favorable risk-to-reward ratio.",
  "merge_notes": "One source is labeled school 'ICT' (SMC-Strong-Weak) while the other two are 'SMC', though all describe the same concept. SMC-Strong-Weak uniquely distinguishes liquidity 'grab' (single-candle false breakout) from 'sweep' (multi-candle accumulation) and adds candle-close/CHoCH confirmation nuances and explicit stop-loss placement, whereas SMC-Extra and SMC-Indicator focus more on the broader sweep-initiation-mitigation sequence into order blocks/FVGs.",
